.TH DF 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
df \- report file system disk space usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B df
.RB [ \-aHhikmPT ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR type ]
.RI [ file " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B df
reports the total, used, and available space of mounted file systems,
as returned by
.BR statvfs (2).
The list of mounts is read from
.BR /proc/mounts .
.PP
With no
.I file
operands, every mounted file system is listed, except those that
report zero total blocks (pseudo file systems) unless
.B \-a
is given.
With operands,
.B df
reports, for each
.IR file ,
the file system whose mount point is the longest path prefix of the
file's resolved absolute path.
.PP
The output has the columns
.BR Filesystem ,
optionally
.BR Type ,
the size,
.BR Used ,
.BR Available ,
.BR Use% ,
and
.BR "Mounted on" .
Each column is as wide as its widest entry.
Sizes are shown in 1024-byte blocks by default.
.B Use%
is used space divided by the sum of used and available space, rounded
up.
If
.BR statvfs (2)
fails for a mount point, its numeric columns show
.BR \- .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Include file systems that report zero blocks.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-human\-readable
Print sizes with a unit suffix
.RB ( K ,
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
.BR T ,
.BR P ,
.BR E )
in powers of 1024.
Values below 10 are shown with one decimal place.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-si
Like
.BR \-h ,
but in powers of 1000.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-inodes
Report inode usage
.RB ( Inodes ,
.BR IUsed ,
.BR IFree ,
.BR IUse% )
instead of block usage.
.TP
.B \-k
Use 1024-byte blocks (the default).
.TP
.B \-m
Use 1048576-byte blocks.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-portability
Use 1024-byte blocks and the POSIX column headings
.B 1024\-blocks
and
.B Capacity
(or
.B ICapacity
with
.BR \-i ).
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-print\-type
Add a column with the file system type.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type= \fItype\fP
Only report file systems of type
.IR type .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.PP
Among
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-k ,
.BR \-m ,
and
.BR \-P ,
the last unit selection given takes effect.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The mount table could not be read, an option was invalid, or no
mount matched a
.I file
operand.
.TP
.B 2
Out of memory.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DF_MOUNTS_FILE
Read the mount table from this file instead of
.B /proc/mounts
(intended for testing).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /proc/mounts
Mount table: device, mount point, and type are taken from the first
three fields of each line.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show all file systems with human-readable sizes and types:
.PP
.EX
$ df \-hT
.EE
.PP
Show the file system holding the current directory:
.PP
.EX
$ df .
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (2),
.BR statvfs (2),
.BR proc (5)
.SH STANDARDS
.B \-k
and
.B \-P
follow POSIX.1-2024.
The remaining options follow GNU coreutils and BSD; in particular
.B \-t
selects a file system type, not the XSI total-space output.
.SH BUGS
If a
.I file
operand cannot be resolved (for example, it does not exist), its name
is matched literally, so a nonexistent absolute path is reported as
belonging to the root file system rather than being diagnosed.
